    body: "North Korea has ordered Switzerland's diplomatic representative to leave the country within 48 hours and called for the office to close amid a row over sanctions.\n\nIt said the move was in response to Switzerland's decision to place sanctions on the Songdowon international children's camp. State media described this as \"a blatant hostile act\".\n\nThat camp is at the centre of claims that it was involved in transferring children from Russian-occupied areas of Ukraine.\n\nNorth Korea's foreign ministry said \"any act of violating the sovereign rights\" of the country and the interests of its citizens \"can never be welcomed\".\n\nSwitzerland is one of the few Western nations to maintain dialogue with Pyongyang.\n\nSwiss authorities said relations between the two countries would be maintained through the North Korean embassy in Bern, and the Swiss embassy in Beijing.\n\nThe diplomatic rebuke comes weeks after Swiss foreign minister Ignazio Cassis made a rare visit to North Korea, where he officially reopened the office and discussed normalising relations.\n\nDiplomatic links with North Korea were first established in 1974 and Switzerland opened a co-operation office in Pyongyang in 1997.\n\nSwitzerland's federal department of foreign affairs said the country \"distinguishes between its sanctions policy and its commitment to maintaining dialogue with all countries\".\n\nIt added: \"The reopening of the office in Pyongyang did not in any way affect Switzerland's implementation of UN sanctions related to North Korea's nuclear programme, nor its implementation of EU sanctions targeting North Korea in the context of Russia's war against Ukraine.\"\n\nThousands of North Korean soldiers have been deployed to help support Russia's war with Ukraine.\n\nIn August, Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky accused Moscow of using North Korean missiles.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published11 August\n\n- Published4 May 2024\n\n- Published14 September",
