    body: "Jeremy Corbyn and Rashida Tlaib join politicians and legal groups condemning Ulm 5 prosecution Submitted by Katherine Hearst on Wed, 11/25/2026 - 13:02 Two letters signed by 26 politicians and 31 legal groups demand the 'immediate release' of defendants in Germany The 'Ulm Five' from left to right: Daniel Tatlow-Devally, Zo Hailu, Crow Tricks, Vi Kovarbasic and Leandra Rollo (supplied) Off Jeremy Corbyn and Rashida Tlaib are among dozens of politicians and legal organisations raising alarm over the prosecution and treatment of pro- Palestine activists in Germany .\n\nTwo letters issued on Wednesday, signed by 26 politicians and 31 legal groups from across the globe, condemned the treatment of five activists who have been held in pretrial detention for over 14 months. Daniel Tatlow-Devally, Zo Hailu, Crow Tricks, Vi Kovarbasic and Leandra Rollo, who are from Germany, Spain , Ireland and the UK , were arrested on 8 September following an alleged break-in at the premises of Israeli arms firm Elbit Systems in the southwestern German city of Ulm.\n\nKnown as the “Ulm Five”, the group is accused of damaging the building's glass facade and smashing office equipment, including computers, screens and telephones. In addition to charges of trespass and damage to property, the five are accused of \"membership of a criminal organisation\", namely Palestine Action Germany, under Section 129 of the German penal code. The legislation is typically used to target organised crime networks and carries a potential sentence of up to five years in prison.\n\nAll the defendants have been held in prolonged pretrial detention, where they report facing restrictive conditions. Under Section 129, suspects can be held for up to six months before trial, though lawyers say this period has now been significantly extended. In their letter, the elected officials - including members of the German Bundestag, the European Parliament, the US Congress, the Irish Parliament and other legislatures across Europe and the Americas - called for the activists’ “immediate release”.\n\nThey further warned that the implications of their prosecution and treatment extend beyond the five defendants, forming “part of an international effort to discipline opposition to war and to place the interests of arms manufacturers above the rights of peoples and the obligations of international law”. 'Politically inconvenient facts' Meanwhile, a separate letter signed by 33 legal groups - including the Center for Constitutional Rights, the Punjab Bar Council, European Democratic Lawyers, the American Association of Jurists, the Irish Council for Civil Liberties, the German Association of Democratic Lawyers - warned that the conduct of the trial could constitute a breach of German and international law.\n\nIn particular, the letter raises concerns about the courtroom’s security arrangements, which it said obstruct confidential communication between the defendants and their lawyers and could undermine the presumption of justice. MEE previously reported that the trial, which began on 27 April, is taking place at a high security courtroom in the notorious Stammheim complex. The defence team has repeatedly protested their clients’ treatment during proceedings, where the defendants are handcuffed and seated behind a glass barrier, preventing confidential communication with their lawyers.\n\nThe legal groups also challenged the court’s rejection of multiple applications by defence lawyers to hear expert witnesses. MEE previously reported that the court refused to hear weapons expert Dr Shir Hever and international law expert Isabel Feichtner, who were presented by the defence team to give evidence regarding Elbit Systems’ role in Israel’s genocide in Gaza and Germany’s obligations under international law.\n\nThe organisations further rejected the use of Section 129 against the defendants, insisting that “the prosecution of specific acts of property damage must not be conflated with the criminalisation of a political protest movement”. \"A criminal trial must establish the relevant facts impartially,\" they urged. \"It must not exclude politically inconvenient facts merely because they concern the conduct of a powerful arms manufacturer or the actions of a state in an ongoing armed conflict\".\n\nIsrael's genocide in Gaza News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
