    title: "US announces new tariffs on host of countries including China",
    summary: "The new levies would hit many of America's biggest trading partners, including China and the EU.",
    preview: "The US has announced a fresh raft of tariffs as President Donald Trump pushes ahead with his signature trade policy. The new taxes charged on imports to America are set to hit many of the country's biggest trading partners - China, the European Union and 12 other jurisdictions. The move follows the White House's investigation into the trade practices of other countries in March after the US Supreme Court ruled Trump's previously announced Liberation Day tariffs were illegal.",
    body: "The US has announced a fresh raft of tariffs as President Donald Trump pushes ahead with his signature trade policy.\n\nThe new taxes charged on imports to America are set to hit many of the country's biggest trading partners - China, the European Union and 12 other jurisdictions.\n\nThe move follows the White House's investigation into the trade practices of other countries in March after the US Supreme Court ruled Trump's previously announced Liberation Day tariffs were illegal.\n\nChina is facing the highest rate of up to 40%, followed by Vietnam at up to 35% and then others facing either 20% or 25%.\n\nThe new duties are not expected to take effect until next year.\n\nTariffs are taxes levied on imported goods. The amount is typically calculated as a percentage of the good's value and paid to the government by businesses importing the products from abroad.\n\nSome of these rates will be effectively reduced because of caps on tariffs Trump has agreed to. Individual countries also face different levies.\n\nThe proposed tariffs will affect China, the European Union, Singapore, Switzerland, Indonesia, Malaysia, Cambodia, Thailand, Korea, Vietnam, Taiwan, Bangladesh, Japan, and India.\n\nUS Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said the latest tariffs issued would \"level the playing field for American workers and chart a new, more sustainable course for the world economy\".\n\nHe claimed that many of America's trading partners had \"for decades\" adopted industrial strategies which included \"targeting manufacturing, wage and labor suppression\" as well as \"other market distorting practices that tilt the trade balance in their favor while eroding America's manufacturing base\".\n\nBut economists have warned tariffs can fuel inflation and push prices up for consumers if businesses pass on higher costs, as well as dampen global trade.\n\nTrump has repeatedly threatened or imposed tariffs during his second term, arguing they will boost US manufacturing and create jobs.\n\nDespite a poor result for the Republican party in the midterm elections, the president has continued to press on with his flagship trade policy.\n\nTrump is also seeking to cut the US trade deficit - the gap between the value of goods it buys from other countries and those it sells to them.\n\nHowever, Stephen Brown, chief North America economist for Capital Economics, said the latest tariff announcement was \"not as alarming as it looks\", due to the taxes \"essentially replacing\" previously announced tariffs made in the summer.\n\n\"The increase in the effective tariff rate looks set to be a touch lower than we've been assuming for our forecasts, although the new tariffs will still provide a marginal boost to prices,\" he said in a note.\n\n\"The key message is that the additional tariff burden is likely to be small, especially as various goods... are also not subject to these new tariffs.\"\n\nThe announcement is likely to increase tensions between the US and China, the world's two biggest economies, who remain locked in a trade dispute and the AI race.\n\nThe two countries have also been embroiled in a tit-for-tat tariffs war, which is currently on hold.\n\nDespite last week's meeting between Trump and China's President Xi Jinping, the two sides made no announcement on the trade war truce, which is set to expire on 10 January.\n\nThe European Commission rejected the new US tariffs, saying it would investigate the US's reasons for bringing them in. It pointed out that tariffs are limited to the 15% previously agreed.\n\n- Published22 August",
